  "result": "The lesson for O3 is written, with a quiz and key ready. I haven't measured the lesson's word count, so I can't confirm it falls inside the 60-minute range (5,400–13,500 words). I estimate around 6,000 words, plus practice time.\n\n**Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html` (60 min). It covers:\n- **Context window:** what the model can see, and what is in it.\n- **Between steps:** your program re-sends the message list on every call, so cost grows with each large result left in.\n- **Full context:** context rot, clearing old tool results, compaction and sub-agents.\n- **Between sessions:** notes as memory, including Anthropic's memory tool, and a fixed lookup versus model-written notes.\n- **Retrieval:** up-front versus just-in-time, and how a manual search tool finds pages.\n- **Where each fact lives:** a four-question rule for deciding where a fact belongs, plus a worked token-cost estimate.\n- **Worked examples:** a worked example and a practice case, then a short plan you draft for your own assistant.\n\nThe primary source is Anthropic's \"Effective context engineering for AI agents\". I also read the memory tool docs. The context-windows docs link was only seen in search results, not opened, and the lesson says so.\n\n**Quiz:** quiz 0002, five items, with its key in `keys/0002.json`. Ask me for it when you want to take it.\n\n**Workspace:** `RESOURCES.md` is new, and `NOTES.md` is updated with the lesson, the quiz and the misconception ids in use. I wrote no cheat sheet this time and no learning record, since nothing has been shown yet.\n\nQuiz 0001 is still ungraded: both attempts, `submissions/s1.json` and `s2.json`, are waiting. Do you want me to grade them next?\n\nYou can ask me about any part of the lesson in the chat.",
